module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_shared_probe_root_lets_one_run_compile_anothers_source

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_shared_probe_root_lets_one_run_compile_anothers_source: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_shared_probe_root_lets_one_run_compile_anothers_source" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a shared probe root lets one run compile another run source** (two executions materialize their generated crate into a directory keyed by WHAT is built rather than by WHO builds it. The second writer overwrites the first; the loser compiles the winner source against its own dependencies, and the type errors are attributed to the loser tree. The exclusion that would stop it EXISTS in this corpus and the failing caller does not take it.)",

    "INVALID STATE: a build whose SOURCE came from one revision and whose DEPENDENCIES came from another, with a diagnostic naming only the second.",

    "THE PROTECTION IS BUILT AND BYPASSED, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THIS ROW. gunbc.emitted_closure_compile_host acquire_probe_root_lock creates an exclusive lock in the probe root and REFUSES a second concurrent run with a typed located cause; its own annotation argues the design -- refusing beats waiting, which serializes into the same shared state, and beats a private directory per run, which throws away the warm target dir. That reasoning is sound and it is already written down. run_required_emit_compile takes the lock. THE v2-NATIVE ROUTE DID NOT: v1_compiler.cli_run.native_lane_runner selected the same root through lane_emit_compile_probe_root and passed it to emission without acquiring anything. So this was not a missing mechanism; it was a mechanism one caller reached around. THAT CALLER IS REPAIRED AS OF 2026-09-21 and the sentence above is kept in the past tense rather than deleted, because the SHAPE is the row's subject and the specimen is what makes it legible.",

    "OCCURRENCE AND REPAIR, 2026-09-21 (zesty-raven-800): THE NAMED BYPASS TAKES THE LOCK. v1_compiler.cli_run.native_lane_runner prepare_emitted_compiler_for_entry now acquires acquire_probe_root_lock BEFORE the emission -- the emission is what WRITES the shared root, so acquiring after would leave that write outside the transaction -- and holds it through the cargo baseline, the injected fault, the restore, the binary-identity re-read AND EVERY SPAWN OF THE ARTIFACT. That last clause is a CORRECTION: the first cut bound the guard locally, so it dropped at the preparation's return and the two entrypoint walks spawned the executable with the lock already free (review 69715). The probe build uses a shared CARGO_TARGET_DIR and the executable's name is derived from the entry, so a peer taking the freed lock could rebuild and replace the binary between the drop and the spawn -- a verdict about somebody else's tree, which is this row's own invalid state reached one step later than the repair covered. The guard now travels with EmittedPreparation and is released when the caller drops it. It is held by a Drop guard rather than a call pair because that function returns early on nine refusal paths, and a leaked lock converts a transient overlap into a permanent refusal for every later run on the host.",

    "WHAT PROMPTED IT IS WORTH RECORDING, because it is this row's mechanism running forward rather than a tidy-up. The same change added a fault-and-restore mutation to that caller -- it deliberately writes a BROKEN emitted tree and then repairs it. Unlocked, that WIDENS exactly the window this row files: a peer's baseline can read the faulted bytes and report Discriminated quoting our injected symbol, or a peer's restore can erase our red before it is read. The lock's own annotation had already MEASURED that shape (a stale background invocation producing a Discriminated verdict over a cargo run whose tail said Finished). So the repair landed in the same change as the hazard that made it urgent.",

    "EVIDENCE, and its limit stated rather than glossed. Executing: a second holder is REFUSED while the first is live, with the cause naming a concurrent run, and the root is re-admissible once the guard drops (v1_compiler.cli_run.native_lane_runner probe_root_exclusion_tests). On the real path, a self-host instrument run prints `probe root /tmp/gunbc-emit-compile held for emit+build+fault+restore`, completes the mutation (Discriminated red=1616) and leaves NO lock file behind. NOT ESTABLISHED: two overlapping preparations with one paused mid-fault. A preparation is a whole-corpus emit plus two cargo builds, so pausing one at a chosen instant was not stageable; what is shown is the property that control would rest on -- refusal rather than admission -- plus release on drop.",

    "THE ROW IS NOT DISCHARGED, exactly as its own trigger below says. Adding the existing call to the one bypassing caller repairs THIS INSTANCE and leaves the next caller free to reach around the same guard: selection and exclusion are still two operations, so the invalid state still has a constructor. The rung for the repaired caller moves from BELOW the ladder to 1, mitigated by a typed refusal, which is where its sibling already sat. The ceiling and the next-rung trigger are unchanged.",

    "WHY IT SURVIVES REVIEW: the guard exists, it is well argued, and a reviewer who greps for it FINDS it. Establishing that a given caller takes it requires following the call, which nobody does when the protection is known to be present. A built wall with one gate left open reads, from a distance, exactly like a built wall.",

    "SPECIMEN (gunbc#10940, 2026-09-12). A baseline run of the base revision and a successor run of the branch executed concurrently on one host. The successor refused EmittedCompilerBuildFailed with E0063 naming three missing fields on a cost-row type. The first reading -- mine -- was that the branch emitted a broken driver. It had not: the emitted main on disk was the BASE revision, written minutes earlier by the baseline, and predated the three fields the branch added. Neither run took the lock, so nothing refused and the wrong verdict was computed against an innocent tree.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACT, so the next reader arrives faster: READ THE GENERATED ARTIFACT. If its content belongs to a revision other than the one being built -- a field set, a call, a spelling from another head -- the build is compiling somebody else source, and its timestamp falling inside another run window confirms it.",

    "THE LOUD FORM WAS ALREADY FIXED AND THE SILENT ONE LEFT OPEN. The same shared directory previously produced EACCES against another job artifacts, and that repair routed the required lane to a declared per-job root. A collision class repaired where it announces itself and left reachable where it does not is the more dangerous half surviving.",

    "RUNG: found BELOW the ladder for the unlocked caller. The run does not merely fail; it fails with a confident, specific cause about the wrong subject. The locked caller sits at 1, mitigated by a typed refusal.",

    "CEILING 4, structurally impossible, and MOST OF THE DISTANCE IS ALREADY TRAVELLED -- which is why the honest ceiling is high rather than aspirational. Selecting the probe root and taking exclusive ownership of it are one operation, so a selector that RETURNS an owned root (the lock taken, released on drop) leaves no spelling by which a caller can hold the path without the exclusion. Then the invalid state has no constructor, rather than depending on each caller remembering.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER (a CAPABILITY, not an artifact): probe-root selection yields an owned root, so acquiring the path and acquiring the exclusion cannot be separated. Adding the existing lock call to the one bypassing caller is a REPAIR of this instance and does NOT discharge the row -- it leaves the next caller free to reach around it the same way. Setting a per-run environment variable is a weaker mitigation still: it protects the launches that remember.",

    "OCCURRENCE, 2026-09-22 (sunny-ibex-112): SAME INVALID STATE, NEW POPULATION -- THE SEED BINARY, not the probe root. /cargo-target is the cargo target directory every session in every worktree resolves by default, so `cargo build -p v1-compiler` writes /cargo-target/release/gunbc and /cargo-target/release/claim_executor for EVERYONE. Keyed by what is built, not by who builds it; second writer wins; the loser measures the winner. That is this row verbatim, one layer up from the emitted probe crate: there the shared artifact is the generated SOURCE, here it is the COMPILER ITSELF.",

    "THE SPECIMEN, and it is the row's own distinguishing fact firing one layer up. A repair to the FreeMonoid match lowering (gunbc#12026) was verified present in the binary by a string that exists only in the repaired code -- count 1. Four enrolled rows were then run. Two returned false. The failing fixture was emitted directly to see why, and the emitted function carried the PRE-REPAIR lowering: a two-way is_empty split, the third arm absent, a head binding referenced but never bound. The binary timestamp fell AFTER the verification. A neighbour had rebuilt v1-compiler from their own mirror in between, and every measurement after that point was a verdict about somebody else's compiler.",

    "WHAT IT NEARLY COST, recorded because the near-miss is the evidence. The output was a textbook reproduction of the very defect under repair, so the available readings were (a) file a finding that the new refusal does not cover refutable head sub-patterns, and repair working code against a phantom, or (b) loosen the two failing assertions until they matched the observed output, which manufactures a green by bending the control -- the worse branch, and the one this corpus exists to refuse. What actually broke the tie was that TWO MEASUREMENTS OF ONE FILE DISAGREED: the repair-only string counted 1 earlier and 0 later, same path. A single measurement is authoritative-looking in either direction.",

    "THREE LANES IN TWO HOURS, which is what moved this from a session mishap to an occurrence. sunny-ibex-112 as above. stern-wolf-590 read DivisionByZero out of a neighbour built binary while probing ?? semantics. bold-seal-329 was warned before it reached them and confirmed their green arm was already built into a session-private target, while noting they could no longer re-verify the red arm's target directory after the fact -- which is itself the reason the artifact-carried form below matters more than the directory discipline.",

    "THE CONSEQUENCE IS WORSE FOR A DIFFERENTIAL INSTRUMENT THAN FOR A SINGLE-ARM LANE, and bold-seal-329's oracle is the case. A single-arm lane gets a false NEGATIVE somebody eventually chases down. A two-arm differential -- interpreter against emitted binary, or broken emitter against repaired emitter -- depends on its arms coming from two SPECIFIC compilers. If a neighbour's build lands between the two emissions, BOTH ARMS BECOME THE SAME COMPILER, the differential reports no divergence, and every field of the record is well formed. The instrument built to catch confident well-formed answers about the wrong subject then produces one about itself.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE, stated as the general form because the directory discipline is the weaker half: PREFER EVIDENCE THAT CARRIES ITS OWN PROVENANCE OVER EVIDENCE THAT DEPENDS ON A TOOL'S STATE AT A MOMENT. A timestamp, a symbol count, or a freshly verified binary is a claim about the INSTRUMENT, and it expires silently the moment a peer writes to the shared path. A retained artifact whose content only one producer could have written is a claim about the SUBJECT, and it stays checkable after the run that made it. Worked example from the same PR: its red control needed no binary check at all, because the baseline crate contains the two-way split and ZERO length-conditions while an enrolled row establishes that the repaired emitter must emit a length test for that exact arm shape -- so the artifact names its own producer. The two refusal rows in that PR were rewritten to the same form after bold-seal-329 asked for it.",

    "THE EXISTING TRIGGER DOES NOT REACH THIS POPULATION, AND THE TRIGGER IS NOT WIDENED TO PRETEND OTHERWISE. That trigger -- probe-root selection yields an owned root, so the path and the exclusion cannot be separated -- works because a selection function exists IN THIS CORPUS to be made to return an owned root. /cargo-target has no such function: the path is chosen by an environment variable set at container setup, outside the corpus, and the writer is cargo itself. There is nothing here for us to make own the exclusion, so the construction that discharges the probe-root population leaves this one standing. Recorded as a scope statement on the row rather than as a broader trigger, because a trigger that names more than a buildable capability is retired by nothing.",

    "AND THE REMEDY THIS OCCURRENCE USED IS THE ONE THIS ROW ALREADY PRICES AS WEAK. Building into a worktree-local CARGO_TARGET_DIR is exactly the row's own `setting a per-run environment variable is a weaker mitigation still: it protects the launches that remember`. It protected this lane only because this lane now remembers, and it protects no caller who does not. Stated plainly so the workaround is not read as a repair. The row's REVIEW TELL also fires cleanly on the new population: grep the protected resource rather than the protection, and every session running a v1-compiler build is a caller of the shared resource that mentions no guard, because none exists on that path.",

    "SECOND OCCURRENCE, 2026-09-23 on srv2, AFTER THE LOCK REPAIR ABOVE -- the class is wider than one unlocked caller. The euid-scoped root <temp>/gunbc-emit-compile-<euid> (v1_compiler.emitted_closure_compile_host local_emit_compile_probe_root, then SELECTED rather than created) was shared by every run of one uid on the host. sleek-cat-104 ran gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:self-host at 6d9d9a4c30e (emit ~00:41); the emitted std_integer.rs it compiled was overwritten at 01:30:41 by another run, and it reported a false E0573 on main after #12089 had already fixed it. Earlier the same day the same directory destroyed an operator's preserved binary. Nothing detected either event: each run reported a result about files it did not write.",

    "DISCHARGE, 2026-09-23 (wise-tern-86): the trigger below is met by construction, not by more locking. The root is no longer selected: v1_compiler.emitted_closure_compile_host create_private_probe_root makes a fresh directory per run under the declared base (RUNNER_TEMP in CI, the system temp locally) with an exclusive mkdir (create_exclusive_probe_root), and PrivateProbeRoot -- private field, so held only by the run that created it -- is the only value every writer in that module accepts. A directory that already exists is refused, never adopted. acquire_probe_root_lock and native_lane_runner ProbeRootLockGuard are DELETED: there is no shared directory left to exclude a peer from. THE EXECUTABLE IS PER-RUN TOO: the cargo target dir was <workspace>/target, where the uplifted binary is named by the package alone, so two runs of one entry in one worktree wrote one executable and a peer could swap it between our identity read and our spawns (review 70338). Every probe build now targets PrivateProbeRoot target_dir inside the run's root; the cost is the three-crate probe dependency graph compiled once per run. A root is removed when its PrivateProbeRoot drops (review 70325: an unremoved per-run root grows the host temp by one emitted crate per local run); PrivateProbeRoot retain is the one explicit keep arm, taken by the claim_executor route that prints the root. Control: emitted_closure_compile_host two_runs_over_different_trees_do_not_share_a_probe_root, two roots from one base, each writing its own tree for one entry; measured RED when create_private_probe_root is swapped back to the euid-keyed derivation, green on the constructor. RESIDUE, stated rather than glossed: the v2-native lane's malformed-specimen materialization and old-route withdrawal still write fixed paths in the worktree (native_lane_runner materialize_malformed_specimen, withdraw_old_route); those are worktree fixtures and the seed binary, not the probe root, and are outside this row's subject.",

    "REVIEW TELL: a guard function whose callers are fewer than the callers of the thing it guards. Grep the protected resource, not the protection, and ask which callers of the resource never mention the guard.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
